{"archive_version": "20260604.144757", "book_slug": "physics", "book_state": "live", "book_title": "Physics", "book_uuid": "cce64fde-f448-43b8-ae88-27705cceb0da", "book_version": "dfdfd7a", "caption": "Relative strength and range of the four fundamental forces", "content_url": "https://openstax.org/apps/archive/20260604.144757/contents/cce64fde-f448-43b8-ae88-27705cceb0da@dfdfd7a:2a6a74b7-53e3-4496-81ef-f40540ffcd84.json", "context": null, "element_id": "Table_23_01", "has_thead": true, "header_cells": [{"col": 0, "row": 0, "scope": "col", "text": "Force"}, {"col": 1, "row": 0, "scope": "col", "text": "Approximate Relative Strength[1]"}, {"col": 2, "row": 0, "scope": "col", "text": "Range"}], "html": "<table id=\"Table_23_01\" data-summary=\"The table consists of three columns with the headers Force, Approximate Relative Strength, and Range. The forces compared are gravity, weak, electromagnetic, and strong. The order of the forces listed is the order of their relative strength, from least to greatest.\"><thead>\n<tr>\n<th scope=\"col\">Force</th>\n<th scope=\"col\">Approximate Relative Strength<sup>[1]</sup></th>\n<th scope=\"col\">Range</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Gravity</td>\n<td> <math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow>\n <mrow>\n  <msup>\n   <mrow>\n    <mn>10</mn></mrow>\n   <mrow>\n    <mn>−38</mn></mrow>\n  </msup>\n  </mrow>\n</mrow><annotation-xml encoding=\"MathML-Content\"><mrow>\n  <msup>\n   <mrow>\n    <mn>10</mn></mrow>\n   <mrow>\n    <mn>−38</mn></mrow>\n  </msup>\n  </mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math>\n</td>\n<td> <math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow>\n <mi>∞</mi>\n</mrow><annotation-xml encoding=\"MathML-Content\"><mi>∞</mi></annotation-xml></semantics></math>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Weak</td>\n<td> <math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow>\n <mrow>\n  <msup>\n   <mrow>\n    <mn>10</mn></mrow>\n   <mrow>\n    <mn>−13</mn></mrow>\n  </msup>\n  </mrow>\n</mrow><annotation-xml encoding=\"MathML-Content\"><mrow>\n  <msup>\n   <mrow>\n    <mn>10</mn></mrow>\n   <mrow>\n    <mn>−13</mn></mrow>\n  </msup>\n  </mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math>\n</td>\n<td> <math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow>\n <mrow>\n  <mo>&lt;</mo><msup>\n   <mrow>\n    <mn>10</mn></mrow>\n   <mrow>\n    <mn>−18</mn></mrow>\n  </msup>\n  <mtext>m</mtext></mrow>\n</mrow><annotation-xml encoding=\"MathML-Content\"><mrow>\n  <mo>&lt;</mo><msup>\n   <mrow>\n    <mn>10</mn></mrow>\n   <mrow>\n    <mn>−18</mn></mrow>\n  </msup>\n  <mtext>m</mtext></mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Electromagnetic</td>\n<td> <math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow>\n <mrow>\n  <msup>\n   <mrow>\n    <mn>10</mn></mrow>\n   <mrow>\n    <mn>−2</mn></mrow>\n  </msup>\n  </mrow>\n</mrow><annotation-xml encoding=\"MathML-Content\"><mrow>\n  <msup>\n   <mrow>\n    <mn>10</mn></mrow>\n   <mrow>\n    <mn>−2</mn></mrow>\n  </msup>\n  </mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math>\n</td>\n<td> <math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow>\n <mi>∞</mi>\n</mrow><annotation-xml encoding=\"MathML-Content\"><mi>∞</mi></annotation-xml></semantics></math>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Strong</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td> <math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow>\n <mrow>\n  <mo>&lt;</mo><msup>\n   <mrow>\n    <mn>10</mn></mrow>\n   <mrow>\n    <mn>−15</mn></mrow>\n  </msup>\n  <mtext>m</mtext></mrow>\n</mrow><annotation-xml encoding=\"MathML-Content\"><mrow>\n  <mo>&lt;</mo><msup>\n   <mrow>\n    <mn>10</mn></mrow>\n   <mrow>\n    <mn>−15</mn></mrow>\n  </msup>\n  <mtext>m</mtext></mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody><tfoot>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\"><sup>[1]</sup>Relative strength is based on the strong force felt by a proton–proton pair.</td>\n</tr>\n</tfoot></table>", "id": "openstax/physics/23-1-the-four-fundamental-forces/tab-001", "index_on_page": 1, "is_layout": false, "kind": "table", "license_name": "Creative Commons Attribution License", "license_url": "http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/", "n_cols": 3, "n_rows": 6, "page_slug": "23-1-the-four-fundamental-forces", "page_title": "23.1 The Four Fundamental Forces", "page_uuid": "2a6a74b7-53e3-4496-81ef-f40540ffcd84", "retrieved_at": "2026-10-08", "role": null, "source": "openstax", "source_url": "https://openstax.org/books/physics/pages/23-1-the-four-fundamental-forces#Table_23_01", "summary": "The table consists of three columns with the headers Force, Approximate Relative Strength, and Range. The forces compared are gravity, weak, electromagnetic, and strong. The order of the forces listed is the order of their relative strength, from least to greatest.", "table_number": "23.1"}
{"archive_version": "20260604.144757", "book_slug": "physics", "book_state": "live", "book_title": "Physics", "book_uuid": "cce64fde-f448-43b8-ae88-27705cceb0da", "book_version": "dfdfd7a", "caption": "Carrier particles and their relative masses compared to pions for the four fundamental forces", "content_url": "https://openstax.org/apps/archive/20260604.144757/contents/cce64fde-f448-43b8-ae88-27705cceb0da@dfdfd7a:2a6a74b7-53e3-4496-81ef-f40540ffcd84.json", "context": null, "element_id": "Table_23_02", "has_thead": true, "header_cells": [{"col": 0, "row": 0, "scope": "col", "text": "Force"}, {"col": 1, "row": 0, "scope": "col", "text": "Carrier Particle"}, {"col": 2, "row": 0, "scope": "col", "text": "Range"}, {"col": 3, "row": 0, "scope": "col", "text": "Relative Strength[1]"}], "html": "<table id=\"Table_23_02\" data-summary=\"The table has four columns for force, carrier particle, range, and relative strength. The four rows of the table are for the four forces: gravity, electromagnetic, weak, and strong. The gravity force has a theorized carrier particle called the graviton and has an infinite range. The electromagnetic force has a carrier particle called the photon and has an infinite range. The weak force has carrier particles called the W and Z bosons and has a range less than 10-18 meters. The strong force has a carrier particle called the Pi Meson, now known as a gluon, and has a range less than 10-15 meters. Relative force values are identical to those listed in [Table_23_01_Force].\"><thead>\n<tr>\n<th scope=\"col\">Force</th>\n<th scope=\"col\">Carrier Particle</th>\n<th scope=\"col\">Range</th>\n<th scope=\"col\">Relative Strength<sup>[1]</sup></th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Gravity</td>\n<td>Graviton (theorized)</td>\n<td> <math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow>\n <mi>∞</mi>\n</mrow><annotation-xml encoding=\"MathML-Content\"><mi>∞</mi></annotation-xml></semantics></math>\n</td>\n<td> <math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow>\n <mrow>\n  <msup>\n   <mrow>\n    <mn>10</mn></mrow>\n   <mrow>\n    <mn>−38</mn></mrow>\n  </msup>\n  </mrow>\n</mrow><annotation-xml encoding=\"MathML-Content\"><mrow>\n  <msup>\n   <mrow>\n    <mn>10</mn></mrow>\n   <mrow>\n    <mn>−38</mn></mrow>\n  </msup>\n  </mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Weak </td>\n<td>W and Z bosons</td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow>\n <mrow>\n  <mo>&lt;</mo><msup>\n   <mrow>\n    <mn>10</mn></mrow>\n   <mrow>\n    <mn>−18</mn></mrow>\n  </msup>\n  <mtext>m</mtext></mrow>\n</mrow><annotation-xml encoding=\"MathML-Content\"><mrow>\n  <mo>&lt;</mo><msup>\n   <mrow>\n    <mn>10</mn></mrow>\n   <mrow>\n    <mn>−18</mn></mrow>\n  </msup>\n  <mtext>m</mtext></mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math>\n</td>\n<td> <math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow>\n <mrow>\n  <msup>\n   <mrow>\n    <mn>10</mn></mrow>\n   <mrow>\n    <mn>−2</mn></mrow>\n  </msup>\n  </mrow>\n</mrow><annotation-xml encoding=\"MathML-Content\"><mrow>\n  <msup>\n   <mrow>\n    <mn>10</mn></mrow>\n   <mrow>\n    <mn>−2</mn></mrow>\n  </msup>\n  </mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Electromagnetic</td>\n<td>Photon </td>\n<td> \n\n\n <math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow>\n <mi>∞</mi>\n</mrow><annotation-xml encoding=\"MathML-Content\"><mi>∞</mi></annotation-xml></semantics></math>\n</td>\n<td> <math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow>\n <mrow>\n  <msup>\n   <mrow>\n    <mn>10</mn></mrow>\n   <mrow>\n    <mn>−13</mn></mrow>\n  </msup>\n  </mrow>\n</mrow><annotation-xml encoding=\"MathML-Content\"><mrow>\n  <msup>\n   <mrow>\n    <mn>10</mn></mrow>\n   <mrow>\n    <mn>−13</mn></mrow>\n  </msup>\n  </mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Strong</td>\n<td>Pi mesons or pions (now known as gluons)</td>\n<td> <math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow>\n <mrow>\n  <mo>&lt;</mo><msup>\n   <mrow>\n    <mn>10</mn></mrow>\n   <mrow>\n    <mn>−15</mn></mrow>\n  </msup>\n  <mtext>m</mtext></mrow>\n</mrow><annotation-xml encoding=\"MathML-Content\"><mrow>\n  <mo>&lt;</mo><msup>\n   <mrow>\n    <mn>10</mn></mrow>\n   <mrow>\n    <mn>−15</mn></mrow>\n  </msup>\n  <mtext>m</mtext></mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math>\n</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody><tfoot>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"4\"><sup>[1]</sup>Relative strength is based on the strong force felt by a proton-proton pair.</td>\n</tr>\n</tfoot></table>", "id": "openstax/physics/23-1-the-four-fundamental-forces/tab-002", "index_on_page": 2, "is_layout": false, "kind": "table", "license_name": "Creative Commons Attribution License", "license_url": "http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/", "n_cols": 4, "n_rows": 6, "page_slug": "23-1-the-four-fundamental-forces", "page_title": "23.1 The Four Fundamental Forces", "page_uuid": "2a6a74b7-53e3-4496-81ef-f40540ffcd84", "retrieved_at": "2026-10-08", "role": null, "source": "openstax", "source_url": "https://openstax.org/books/physics/pages/23-1-the-four-fundamental-forces#Table_23_02", "summary": "The table has four columns for force, carrier particle, range, and relative strength. The four rows of the table are for the four forces: gravity, electromagnetic, weak, and strong. The gravity force has a theorized carrier particle called the graviton and has an infinite range. The electromagnetic force has a carrier particle called the photon and has an infinite range. The weak force has carrier particles called the W and Z bosons and has a range less than 10-18 meters. The strong force has a carrier particle called the Pi Meson, now known as a gluon, and has a range less than 10-15 meters. Relative force values are identical to those listed in [Table_23_01_Force].", "table_number": "23.2"}
